**Theorem 9.1 (A nonconstant base arc of original strict $M$ pairs).**
There is a real analytic one-parameter family of strict-real distinct
$M$ pairs, using all original clocks and a common off-$24$ base,
whose acquired base image is infinite. In particular
$\dim\mathcal B_M\ge1$.

Proof. Reuse the original strict pair in SV, Section 7. Its rational
response map is precisely (2.7), (1.8), and (1.9), with

$$
e=-\frac{24}{5}(b-1/8)(6/T-2/U)/\Lambda
\tag{9.1}
$$

on its regular source neighborhood. Let $u=r_A$ and
$z=(b_A,r_B,b_B)$, and form the three actual differences

$$
F(u,z)=(p_A-p_B,\ v_A-v_B,\ m_A-m_B).
\tag{9.2}
$$

At $u_0=99/100$ the certified source cube has center

$$
\bar z=(0.895717129219469386,\
0.938647204769207016,\
0.875817481084238252),\qquad
\|z-\bar z\|_\infty\le10^{-10}.
\tag{9.3}
$$

Every terminating decimal denotes its exact rational value. The
preconditioner in that result is

$$
R_*=10^{-8}
\begin{pmatrix}
-546967580&154697260&-16273831\\
-1318809200&516408400&-77174079\\
-1041709400&350131100&-45748945
\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det R_*=
\frac{2835442353395849767}{500000000000000000000}\ne0.
\tag{9.4}
$$

Its exact rational bounds on the cube are

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{rowsum}_i(I-R_*D_zF)
&<\left(\frac{25559}{10^8},\frac{71315}{10^8},
\frac{51557}{10^8}\right)_i,\\
\|R_*F(u_0,\bar z)\|_\infty&<3/10^{19}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{9.5}
$$

In particular the row-sum norm is less than $1/1000$.
The contraction $z\mapsto z-R_*F(u_0,z)$ maps the cube strictly
inside itself, since $3/10^{19}+10^{-13}<10^{-10}$.
Its fixed point $z_*$ is an actual zero of $F$, because $R_*$
is invertible. These are the existing SV bounds, applied to their
original response circuit.

At this zero the source and response enclosures from the same
certificate include

$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
&A&B\\ \hline
r&99/100&(0.938647,0.938648)\\
b&(0.895717,0.895718)&(0.875817,0.875818)\\
j&(0.906769,0.906770)&(0.941571,0.941572)\\
n&(1.896769,1.896770)&(1.880218,1.880219)\\
\Lambda&(-1.996844,-1.996843)&(-1.813603,-1.813602)\\
e&(4.536296,4.536297)&(4.911799,4.911800)
\end{array}
\tag{9.6}
$$

and

$$
57.024793<p<57.024796,\qquad
304.771484<v<304.771493,\qquad
1227.801824<m<1227.801848.
\tag{9.7}
$$

The matrix bound in (9.5) makes $R_*D_zF(u_0,z_*)$
invertible, by the convergent Neumann series. Hence $D_zF$ is
invertible there. All original response functions are analytic near
these strict regular sources. The analytic implicit-function theorem
therefore gives $z=z(u)$ for $u$ in an interval about $99/100$,
with $F(u,z(u))=0$. This uses the classical analytic theorem;
see Krantz–Parks, *The Implicit Function Theorem*.

Shrink the interval so the strict inequalities and nonzero
denominators in (9.6) persist, $r_A>r_B$, and $p\ne24$ by
(9.7). Formula (2.7) reconstructs one whole nonzero degree-at-most-three
gain and one fixed drift in each label for every parameter. The
parent identity (2.3) gives every original clock, including clock
four. The positive real slacks (2.9), the inverse squared source
distance, and $1/(p-24)$ provide the literal guards (1.9)–(1.10).
Thus this is an actual strict-real arc inside $\mathcal Z_M$,
not merely an arc of common algebraic indices.

If its acquired base image were finite, continuity on a connected
interval would make $(p(u),v(u))$ constant. But F, Theorem 32.1,
bounds every fixed actual off-$24$ child by twenty-four complete
worlds, whereas the first label of this arc has $r_A=u$ and hence
gives infinitely many distinct complete worlds. This is impossible.
The base image is infinite. A zero-dimensional complex affine
algebraic set is finite, so its Zariski closure has dimension at
least one. It lies in $\mathcal B_M$, proving the lower bound. ∎

